preConditionHook:
// A SCALP TARGET, BUT ONLY ON A WEAK BREAK. When the bar the trade was entered
// on had a range smaller than minBreakAtrMultiple ATRs -- a slow, unconvincing
// break rather than a decisive one -- Brooks caps the trade at a scalper's
// profit instead of holding for a swing. On any other trade the rule never
// fires and whatever swing target is placed alongside it stands alone.
//
// THE ANCHOR IS THE ENTRY BAR, not T17-07's "trend-line break bar", which would
// drag the whole trend-line machinery into an exit. The merge pass made that
// substitution deliberately -- in the setup Brooks describes they are the same
// bar -- and it is what makes the rule authorable at all.
//
// THE RANGE IS FROZEN ON THE FILL BAR, where ctx.BarsSinceEntry() == 0
// (VERIFIED: 0 on the fill bar, 0 while flat, restarting through a reversal
// without passing through flat). Reading high[ctx.BarsSinceEntry()] every bar
// instead, as the merged pseudocode spells it, would make the deepest bar index
// the length of the longest trade and so make Max Bars Back unknowable. While
// flat the capture simply repeats on the current bar, which is harmless: the
// last capture before a position runs IS the fill bar's, and the MarketPosition
// guard in the conditions is what makes the rule an exit.
//
// NOT AtrProfitTarget, which always exits at a multiple of ATR in profit: this
// exits at a FIXED TICK target and only when the entry bar was small, so it is
// a conditional target rather than a scaled one, and the two fire on disjoint
// sets of trades whenever the entry bar was large. Not BreakoutMissesScalpTarget,
// which is about failing to reach a target rather than taking one.
//
// SIDES ARE IDENTICAL: every term is phrased in profit, in a bar's own range,
// or in ATR, so there is nothing to mirror -- the StopLoss convention.
//
// ATR is MultiWalk's WFSafe_AvgTrueRange, the ROLLING accumulator: seeded with a
// full sum on the first calculated bar and on a length change, then advanced as
// TWO statements, because EL evaluates S[1] + in - out left to right and folding
// it to S += in - out rounds differently (@OJ 2015-02-20). AtrProfitTarget is
// the reference with the measurements; one call site, here.
//
// OpenPositionProfit is NET of the entry side's costs (VERIFIED), which is what
// every target in this corpus thresholds on, and BigPointValue converts the tick
// target to the same currency.
// THE TICK is EL's own MinMove / PriceScale, both VERIFIED and per-symbol
// constants for the whole run, written DIVISION-FIRST in both twins so the C++
// reads token for token against its EasyLanguage. Read once into a local.
//
// Scratch below is declared in localVariables, not at hook scope: the generator
// only prefixes declared locals, so a hook-scope declaration is a redefinition
// when this rule is placed twice in one strategy. Every one is fully assigned
// each bar before it is read.
tickSz = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
if (atrLength != (int)atrLookback) {
    atrSum = 0.0;
    for (int i = 0; i < (int)atrLookback; ++i) {
        double hi = high[i] > close[i + 1] ? high[i] : close[i + 1];
        double lo = low[i] < close[i + 1] ? low[i] : close[i + 1];
        atrSum += hi - lo;
    }
    atrLength = (int)atrLookback;
} else {
    const int out = (int)atrLookback;
    double hiIn = high[0] > close[1] ? high[0] : close[1];
    double loIn = low[0] < close[1] ? low[0] : close[1];
    double hiOut = high[out] > close[out + 1] ? high[out] : close[out + 1];
    double loOut = low[out] < close[out + 1] ? low[out] : close[out + 1];
    atrSum += hiIn - loIn;
    atrSum -= hiOut - loOut;
}
atrVal = atrSum / (int)atrLookback;
if (ctx.BarsSinceEntry() == 0) {
    entryRange = high[0] - low[0];
}
